POW.SYSTEMS =E2=80=94 Post=E2=80=91AGI Physical Scarcity + Catabolic Capita=
l Thesis

Date: 20 September 2026
Executive thesis

POW should be positioned as the live economic graph of the physical world
that intelligence cannot instantly commoditize.

Our post=E2=80=91AGI premise was already: when cognition/software becomes c=
heap,
scarcity migrates toward physical activation, authoritative world state,
failure histories, qualification/permission, power, cooling, physical
feedback, calibration, repair capacity, and the ability to make real
objects actually work.

Greer adds a second axis: civilization does not only build capital; it has
to maintain an ever-larger installed base. When the cost of maintenance
rises faster than surplus, systems do not disappear overnight. They degrade
selectively, get patched, cannibalized, repaired, substituted, regionalized
and abandoned at the margin.

POW should combine both:

*Post=E2=80=91AGI:* what remains scarce when intelligence is abundant?
*Greer:* which parts of the installed physical system become harder/more
valuable to maintain?
*POW:* measure those scarcity gradients continuously before they become
obvious.

A useful formulation:

Physical Alpha =3D
(importance =C3=97 scarcity =C3=97 qualification =C3=97 failure frequency =
=C3=97 replacement
friction)
=C3=B7 (substitutability =C3=97 software-replicability)

And the durable moat is not the formula. It is the historical dataset.
The reframing

POW is not primarily:

   - a crypto dashboard
   - a mining calculator
   - a content site
   - a repair marketplace
   - an AI-news site

POW is:

*A physical-scarcity observatory: a time-series graph of machines,
components, sensors, energy, failures, maintenance, repairability,
qualified capacity and substitution.*

Crypto/mining remains a perfect first vertical because it exposes physical
compute economics unusually clearly: machine price, hashrate, power price,
thermals, network demand, issuance, depreciation and resale are all
observable.

But the same kernel extends into:

   - sensors and instrumentation
   - actuators
   - 3D printers / local fabrication
   - robotics
   - solar / batteries / inverters
   - electronics and PCB supply
   - server / GPU / home-lab hardware
   - industrial controls
   - EV / vehicle failure ecology
   - eventually HVAC, pumps, motors and trades equipment

Why AGI makes this more valuable, not less

If model intelligence becomes near-free, software analysis rapidly
commoditizes. An agent can write code, read manuals and reason about
repairs.

But the agent still needs reality.

It needs to know:

   - what exact physical object exists
   - where it is
   - what state it is in now
   - which component failed
   - which replacement part is actually available
   - whether a substitute is compatible
   - who is permitted/qualified to install it
   - how often that repair works
   - whether a supplier really has stock
   - what the local electricity / thermal / logistics constraint is
   - whether an old machine is cheaper to maintain than replace
   - how rapidly those facts decay

This is the old WorldStateReliability concept:

WorldStateReliability =3D
identity certainty =C3=97 state freshness =C3=97 provenance =C3=97 permissi=
on

POW should accumulate exactly the reality layer future agents need.
Greer =C3=97 post=E2=80=91AGI: the surprising market

The major market may be *maintenance intelligence for a rapidly expanding
technological installed base*.

AGI/robotics may cause a burst of physical capital creation:

   - robots
   - machine vision
   - cameras
   - environmental sensors
   - smart meters
   - actuators
   - edge computers
   - GPUs
   - inverters
   - batteries
   - EV chargers
   - 3D printers
   - local microfactories

Every installed device is a future maintenance claim.

Therefore:

automation boom
=E2=86=92 installed physical capital explodes
=E2=86=92 failure population grows
=E2=86=92 parts/support/software age
=E2=86=92 repair and substitution become economically meaningful
=E2=86=92 maintenance telemetry becomes valuable

That is pure Greer, but AGI accelerates the creation of the capital stock
that later needs maintaining.
The POW graph

Every physical object should eventually fit roughly:

Asset
=E2=86=92 Model / identity
=E2=86=92 Components
=E2=86=92 Sensors / state
=E2=86=92 Energy requirement
=E2=86=92 Failure modes
=E2=86=92 Repair operations
=E2=86=92 Replacement parts
=E2=86=92 Compatible substitutes
=E2=86=92 Market prices
=E2=86=92 Installed-base estimate
=E2=86=92 Qualification / permission requirements
=E2=86=92 Local service capacity
=E2=86=92 Outcome history
=E2=86=92 Scrap / donor-part value

The atomic economic event is not =E2=80=9Cnews.=E2=80=9D It is a *state tra=
nsition*.

Examples:

   - component goes out of stock
   - lead time doubles
   - printer failure rate rises
   - firmware support ends
   - used price falls below donor-part value
   - electricity price crosses machine breakeven
   - recall appears
   - failure reports cluster around one subsystem
   - replacement component becomes available from new supplier
   - a machine gains open firmware
   - repair success improves
   - certified installer population fails to keep pace with deployments

Those are machine-readable POW events.
The highest-value gardens to plant now 1. Electronic component scarcity
garden

Start immediately.

Use Nexar/Octopart + DigiKey.

Nexar currently exposes supply-chain data across tens of millions of
electronic components, including stock, pricing, lifecycle and lead-time
data updated daily. DigiKey Product Information V4 exposes product
search/details and real-time pricing/availability through authenticated
APIs.

Collect hourly/daily snapshots for selected families:

   - power electronics
   - MOSFETs / IGBTs
   - motor drivers
   - current sensors
   - encoders
   - temperature / pressure / vibration sensors
   - ADC/DAC
   - industrial comms
   - MCUs
   - power supplies
   - fans
   - bearings where distributor data exists
   - connector families
   - 3D printer control electronics
   - mining/compute PSU-related parts

Do not attempt everything. Track the =E2=80=9Cboring indispensable=E2=80=9D=
 components with
high downstream gating leverage.

Sources:
Nexar API: https://nexar.com/api
Nexar developer docs: https://nexar.com/developer
DigiKey API: https://developer.digikey.com/
DigiKey Product Information V4:
https://developer.digikey.com/products/product-information-v4

Critical derived metrics:

   - stock_decay_rate
   - lead_time_acceleration
   - supplier_concentration
   - price_elasticity_to_stock
   - lifecycle_risk
   - substitution_count
   - qualification_debt
   - component_content_per_target_machine

This is almost exactly the post=E2=80=91AGI =E2=80=9CO-ring bottleneck=E2=
=80=9D garden.
2. Repair/failure garden

Open Repair Alliance is extraordinarily useful as a seed dataset: its
latest published aggregate contains 305,649 rows of electrical/electronic
repair attempts, licensed CC BY-SA 4.0.

Source:
https://openrepair.org/open-data/downloads/

iFixit provides structured repair guides, including device/component
hierarchy, tools, difficulty and repair procedures. Non-commercial API
access is free; commercial access requires contacting iFixit.

Sources:
https://www.ifixit.com/News/3981/the-worlds-first-repair-api
https://www.ifixit.com/Info/Repair_Guide

This gives POW a starting failure ontology:
device =E2=86=92 component =E2=86=92 symptom =E2=86=92 intervention =E2=86=
=92 difficulty =E2=86=92 tools =E2=86=92 outcome

Then POW should collect its OWN outcome layer wherever possible. That
proprietary outcome history is much more valuable than copying guides.

Metrics:

   - failure_capture_ratio
   - component_failure_frequency
   - repair_success_probability
   - median repair time
   - spare_part_availability
   - donor_part_value
   - economically_repairable_probability
   - repair_vs_replace_spread

3. 3D printer / local fabrication telemetry garden

This could be a sleeper.

3D printers are tiny cyberphysical factories. In a post=E2=80=91AGI world t=
hey are
an early laboratory for local autonomous manufacturing.

OctoPrint already exposes printer state, temperatures, job state and
temperature history via its API.

Source:
https://docs.octoprint.org/en/main/api/printer.html

Moonraker exposes persistent print-job history, including lists queryable
by time.

Source:
https://moonraker.readthedocs.io/en/latest/external_api/history/

Klipper maintains a large open configuration corpus for mainstream
printers, boards, kits and kinematic types.

Source:
https://github.com/Klipper3d/klipper/blob/master/docs/Example_Configs.md

POW opportunity:
create an opt-in tiny adapter/plugin that records anonymized:

   - printer model
   - board / hotend / extruder / sensor config
   - material
   - job length
   - success/failure
   - error state
   - temp traces
   - maintenance events
   - replaced component
   - print hours before failure
   - energy if available
   - repair outcome

The garden compounds into:
*real-world machine reliability by model/component/configuration.*

That could later power agents deciding:

   - which printer to buy
   - which machine to route a job to
   - preventive maintenance
   - whether a used machine is mispriced
   - which spare parts to hold
   - which designs are robust to local fabrication

This is exactly the kind of data that cannot be recreated retrospectively
once the world realizes it wants it.
4. Sensor truth / calibration garden

Public sensor APIs are useful less because POW needs to become a weather
service and more because they provide a sandbox for modelling *sensor
identity, drift, availability, provenance and physical truth bandwidth*.

OpenAQ v3 exposes locations, instruments, manufacturers, sensors and
historical measurements.

Sources:
https://docs.openaq.org/api
https://docs.openaq.org/resources/sensors

UK Environment Agency flood-monitoring API exposes monitoring stations and
near-real-time water levels/flows, often measured at roughly 15-minute
intervals.

Source:
https://environment.data.gov.uk/flood-monitoring/doc/reference

USGS=E2=80=99s modernized Water Data APIs expose continuous real-time senso=
r
measurements, daily values and station/time-series metadata in
machine-readable formats.

Source:
https://www.usgs.gov/tools/usgs-water-data-apis

Potential POW research:

   - sensor dropout
   - measurement disagreement
   - maintenance gaps
   - station age
   - calibration proxies
   - failure/recovery events
   - geographic undersensing
   - value of redundant sensors

This teaches the kernel how to model reality reliability before POW
eventually ingests private industrial sensor streams.
5. UK energy / physical-compute constraint garden

Elexon=E2=80=99s Insights Solution APIs are public, production-grade REST A=
PIs and
currently require no API key.

Source:
https://developer.data.elexon.co.uk/

NESO=E2=80=99s data portal exposes CKAN APIs and datasets including 2026 hi=
storic
demand, wind/solar and interconnector outturn.

Sources:
https://www.neso.energy/data-portal/api-guidance
https://www.neso.energy/data-portal/historic-demand-data/historic_demand_da=
ta_2026

Collect:

   - system demand
   - imbalance
   - generation mix
   - wind/solar outturn
   - constraint events
   - regional power proxies where available
   - wholesale price relationships
   - compute/mining breakeven overlays

The point is not a prettier energy dashboard.

The point is to learn:
*which physical compute/industrial activities become viable at which energy
states?*
6. Used-equipment / donor-part market garden

eBay Browse API supports search by keyword/category/GTIN/product and
filters including condition and price.

Source:
https://developer.ebay.com/api-docs/buy/api-browse.html

Collect repeated snapshots of:

   - machine model
   - asking price
   - condition
   - parts-only listings
   - refurbished listings
   - component listings
   - listing disappearance
   - geographical supply where permitted

Important limitation: active listing data is not the same thing as reliable
realized transaction data. POW should explicitly label observability rather
than pretending asking prices are sale prices.

But repeated snapshots can still reveal:

   - supply surges
   - depreciation
   - parts-only ratios
   - model-specific failure ecology
   - donor-part floors
   - repair-vs-replace spreads

7. Regulated failure datasets as a template

NHTSA is an excellent example of what the mature POW failure graph should
look like. Its APIs/data expose recalls, complaints and manufacturer
communications; complaints can be queried by model/year/make, and bulk data
is downloadable.

Source:
https://www.nhtsa.gov/nhtsa-datasets-and-apis

In the UK, DVSA=E2=80=99s MOT history API provides vehicle/test/recall data=
, and
MOT histories include defects/failures for vehicles.

Source:
https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/dvsa-privacy-notices/mot-history=
-api-privacy-notice

OPSS publishes UK product safety alerts, reports and recalls.

Source:
https://www.gov.uk/guidance/product-recalls-and-alerts

These sources demonstrate the value of longitudinal failure traces.

POW eventually wants NHTSA-like failure intelligence for:

   - miners
   - GPUs/PSUs
   - solar inverters
   - home batteries
   - 3D printers
   - robots
   - sensors
   - industrial electronics

There is no universal regulator forcing those datasets into existence. That
absence is the opportunity.
The new category POW can own

I would define the category as:

*Physical Technology Intelligence*

Not IoT.
Not repair.
Not industrial analytics.
Not market data.

POW answers:

What physical technologies exist, what do they depend on, what state are
they in, what is getting scarce, what is failing, what can substitute for
it, and where is the economic value moving?

That lets POW cover things as different as:

